@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

Pimacs is an Emacs client for @uref{https://pi.dev/, Pi Coding Agent}.
It provides an interactive interface to the Pi Coding Agent directly
from Emacs.

@node Setup
@chapter Setup

@section Install the Pi Agent

First, install the Pi agent using npm:

@example
npm install -g --ignore-scripts @@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent
@end example

Pimacs does not yet support authentication configuration.  To configure your provider, run @code{pi} in a terminal, then run @code{/login} in Pi.

@section Install @code{pimacs.el}

Pimacs is available from @uref{https://stable.melpa.org/, MELPA Stable}, which
is recommended for installation.  Add it to your package archives, then install
with @code{use-package}:

@lisp
(add-to-list 'package-archives
             '("melpa-stable" . "https://stable.melpa.org/packages/") t)

(use-package pimacs
  :ensure t)
@end lisp

@node Doctor
@chapter Doctor

Run @kbd{M-x pimacs-doctor} to display a dependency report.  It checks that
Pi is available and meets the minimum supported version, and that Tree-sitter
and the Markdown grammars are installed.  When Pi or either grammar is
missing, the report provides a button to install it.

@node Usage
@chapter Usage

Run @kbd{M-x pimacs-chat} from any file in your project to start a Pimacs chat
session.  Use a prefix argument (@kbd{C-u M-x pimacs-chat}) to open a
transient where you can set a session name and root directory.  Setting a root
directory starts the chat there instead of using @code{project.el} to find the
project root.  Run @kbd{M-x pimacs-switch-session} to switch between any
active chat sessions.

The chat buffer is read-only except for the prompt input area.  Use
@kbd{RET} to submit a prompt and @kbd{C-j} to insert a newline.  Press
@kbd{i} from anywhere in the chat buffer to move point to the prompt
input area.

