@tab
@tab Set thinking level.
@end multitable

@node File Name Completion
@section File Name Completion

Type @kbd{@@} followed by a partial file path to trigger file name
completion in the prompt buffer.  The completion backend is controlled
by @code{pimacs-file-completion-backend}.

@node Bash
@section Bash

Prefix a command with @code{!} to run it in Bash.  Use @code{!!} to
run a command without adding it to the conversation context.

@example
! echo "hello"
@end example

@node Sending Context
@section Sending Context

You can send contextual information from any buffer to the Pimacs chat
prompt.

@multitable @columnfractions .35 .65
@headitem Command @tab Description
@item @code{pimacs-send-region} @tab Append the selected region to the prompt input.
@item @code{pimacs-send-filename} @tab Append the current buffer's filename (prefixed with @code{@@}) to the prompt input.
@item @code{pimacs-send-flycheck-errors} @tab Append the Flycheck errors at point to the prompt input.  Falls back to all current errors in the buffer when there are none at point.
@end multitable

@node Images
@section Images

You can attach images to your prompt to provide visual context to the
agent.  Images are displayed as thumbnail previews above the prompt
input area and are sent along with your message.  The agent may also
render images inline in its responses, for example when using the
@code{read} tool on a file that contains images.

@subsection Supported Formats

The following image formats are supported:

@itemize @bullet
@item PNG
@item JPEG
@item GIF
@item WebP
@end itemize


Drag an image file from your file manager and drop it into the Pimacs
chat buffer, or paste a supported image from the clipboard via
@code{yank-media}.  To remove an attached image, click on the
thumbnail with the mouse, or place point on it and press
@kbd{backspace} or @kbd{delete}.

@node State Lines
@section State Lines

The header line displays session context and model information.  The
mode line displays the current agent state.  Customize them with the
@code{pimacs-header-line-format} and @code{pimacs-mode-line-format}
defcustoms.  Both accept strings and state components; use
@code{:spacer} to right-align following components.

@example
(setq pimacs-header-line-format
      '(:context_usage :spacer
        (:model face font-lock-function-name-face)))
@end example

See @code{pimacs-header-line-format} for available components.  A
component may also be a function that receives the current state plist.

Extension status text can be placed in either state line with a
@code{(:status STATUS-KEY ...)} component.  STATUS-KEY is the key used by
the extension's status update.  Text properties after the key customize the
status at that placement.

@example
(setq pimacs-header-line-format
      '(:context_usage " " (:status "xyz-status" face font-lock-warning-face)
        :spacer :model))
@end example

By default, status text also appears beside the prompt.  To display a status
only in a state line, add its key to @code{pimacs-status-widget-hidden-keys}.

@example
(setq pimacs-status-widget-hidden-keys '("xyz-status"))
@end example

@node Managing Sessions
@section Managing Sessions

Use @code{pimacs-switch-session} to switch between active Pimacs chat
sessions using completion.  Use @code{pimacs-list-sessions} to display all
active chat sessions in a sortable tabulated list.  Press @kbd{RET} to visit
the session on the current line and @kbd{g} to refresh the list.  Select a
column header to sort by that column.

Customize the list's columns and initial sort order with
@code{pimacs-list-sessions-table} and @code{pimacs-list-sessions-sort-key}.

@node Section Visibility
@section Section Visibility

Pimacs represents the chat as a hierarchy of sections.  Every non-root section
has a type, a visibility state, and may have a parent or children.  Put point in
a section and run @kbd{M-x pimacs-describe-section} to open a Help buffer with
these details.  The report shows the section's @code{type}, visibility and
user override state, automatic-hiding eligibility, applied face, buffer
positions, and metadata.  Parent and child section types are links; use
@kbd{RET} to follow them.  The @code{type} value is the symbol to use when
configuring section faces or automatic hiding, such as @code{tool-call}.

Use @kbd{TAB} or @kbd{C-i} to toggle the section at point.  Hiding a section
keeps its first line visible, so it can be shown again.  On graphical displays,
the fringe indicator can also be selected with the mouse to toggle visibility.
Use @kbd{S-TAB} to cycle the visibility of the entire chat, or @kbd{1},
@kbd{2}, and @kbd{3} to show progressively more of the tree around point.
@kbd{M-1}, @kbd{M-2}, and @kbd{M-3} do the same for every top-level section.
See @ref{Keybindings} for the complete list of visibility and navigation
commands.

As a conversation grows, Pimacs automatically hides older top-level sections.
@code{pimacs-section-autohide-count} sets how many eligible recent sections
remain visible; its default is 2, and @code{nil} disables automatic hiding.
Use @code{pimacs-section-autohide-filter} to select the eligible section
types.  For example, the following keeps four eligible sections visible while
never automatically hiding user sections:

@lisp
(setq pimacs-section-autohide-count 4
      pimacs-section-autohide-filter '(:exclude user))
@end lisp

Automatic hiding does not override a section whose visibility you explicitly
toggled with @kbd{TAB} or @kbd{C-i}.
Set @code{pimacs-section-visibility-indicators} to @code{nil} to disable the
fringe indicators.

@node UI Customization
@chapter UI Customization

Pimacs renders the chat as a sequence of sections.  Each section type has a
face, so the chat can be styled without changing the Markdown renderer.
Customize @code{pimacs-section-type-faces} to select the face for a section
type, and customize the selected face with @kbd{M-x customize-face}.

The built-in section faces are listed in @ref{Custom Faces}.

@section Common Styling Effects

A section face supplies the base appearance for one kind of chat content.
Only the attributes that you set take effect; leave an attribute unspecified
when the Markdown or role styling should continue to provide it.

For a coloured assistant card that preserves Markdown colours, set only a
background and @code{:extend t}:

@lisp
(set-face-attribute 'pimacs-section-assistant-face nil
                    :background "#18212b"
                    :extend t)
@end lisp

Set @code{:foreground} when every part of a section should use one colour.
Otherwise, leave it as @code{unspecified} to retain Markdown heading, link,
and syntax colours.  Likewise, set @code{:height} or @code{:slant} to make a
section compact or italic, and leave @code{:weight} unspecified when Markdown
bold text should remain bold.

Role labels such as @code{assistant>} and tool names retain their own
recognizable colours by default.  Configure their faces separately when you
want to change them.

@section Cards and Padding

Section faces are also applied to @code{pimacs-section-padding}.  Specify
both @code{:background} and @code{:extend t} for a full-width card background;
the background then also covers empty padding lines.

Thinking defaults to @code{shadow}.  A compact, more subdued thinking card
can be configured as follows:

@lisp
(set-face-attribute 'pimacs-section-thinking-face nil
                    :foreground "#64707c"
                    :background "#131a21"
                    :height 0.9
                    :extend t)
@end lisp

@section Choosing Section Faces

The type-to-face mapping is evaluated when a section is created.  Change one
entry without replacing the other defaults with @code{alist-get}:

@lisp
(setf (alist-get 'tool-result pimacs-section-type-faces)
      'my-pimacs-tool-result-face)
@end lisp

Define a named face when several attributes belong together:

@lisp
(defface my-pimacs-tool-result-face
  '((t :inherit fixed-pitch
       :foreground "#c2cbd5"
       :background "#1a2027"
       :extend t))
  "My Pimacs tool result face.")
@end lisp

Changing a face updates already-displayed text immediately.  Changing
@code{pimacs-section-type-faces} affects newly created sections; recreate the
chat contents to apply a new mapping to existing sections.

@section Prompt Face

The editable portion of the prompt uses @code{pimacs-prompt-face} and can be
customized independently:

@lisp
(set-face-attribute 'pimacs-prompt-face nil
                    :foreground "#d7e0e8"
                    :background "#202936"
                    :extend t)
@end lisp

The @code{user>} prompt label uses @code{pimacs-chat-user-role-face}.

@node Sandbox
@chapter Sandbox

To run Pi inside a sandbox, customize @code{pimacs-executable} and
@code{pimacs-flags}:

@lisp
(setq pimacs-executable "nono")
(setq pimacs-flags '("run" "--silent" "--profile" "pi" "--allow-cwd" "--" "pi"
                 "--tools" "read,bash,edit,write,grep,find,ls"))
@end lisp

@node How It Works
@chapter How It Works

Emacs starts Pi in @uref{https://pi.dev/docs/latest/rpc, RPC mode}.
In this setup, Pi handles the agent logic while Emacs provides the
user interface. Some features, such as @code{/login} and
@code{/logout}, are not
@uref{https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/issues/885, supported}
because they are not currently exposed through the RPC API.

@section Extension UI

Pimacs supports the RPC-compatible extension UI methods: notifications,
selection, confirmation, text input, editing text in an Emacs buffer, setting
the prompt text and chat title, prompt widgets, and status text.  Extension
commands, tools, hooks, and custom messages are also supported.

Pimacs does not support terminal-specific extension UI features, including
@code{ctx.ui.custom()} components, component factories, extension keyboard
shortcuts, or custom TUI rendering callbacks.  Extensions should use the
supported UI methods when running in RPC mode.  See the
@uref{https://pi.dev/docs/latest/extensions, Pi extension documentation} for
the full extension API.

